ct negative examples. Consequently, the spectral regularizer belongs to the dimension-contrastive family in Garrido et al. (2022) , which is provably dual to sample‐contrastive learning with positives and negatives. From this viewpoint, for a given perturbed sample as an anchor, instances perturbed from different clean examples can be interpreted as negatives, whereas instances perturbed from the same clean example play the role of positives. Interestingly, in its dual (sample-contrastive) form, our spectral regularizer admits a reformulation as diffusion score distillation ( Poole et al., 2022 ) . Proposition 4.3 . Minimizing the self-supervised learning objective in Equation 21 via a gradient-based optimizer is equivalent to minimizing the KL divergence D KL ( p t ψ θ ( 𝐱 t ) ∥ p + ) D_{	ext{KL}}(p_{t}^{\psi_{	heta}}( m{x}_{t})~\lVert~p_{+}) , as the following identity shows: ∂ ℒ s ∂ θ \displaystyle rac{\partial\mathcal{L}_{s}}{\partial	heta} = 𝔼 𝒙 ∼ p t ​ [ ( ∇ θ ψ θ ​ ( 𝒙 , t ) ) ⊤ ​ ∇ ψ θ ​ ( 𝒙 , t ) ℒ s ] \displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{ m{x}\sim p_{t}}\left[\left(
